 Saint-Maurice d'Agaune is a monastery in the Rhône valley of the Swiss canton of Valais, built at the traditional site of the martyrdom of Saint Maurice…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-medard"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abbey-of-saint-medard.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abbey of Saint-Médard</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Abbey of Saint-Médard was a Benedictine monastery at Soissons in northern France, founded in the 6th century over the tomb of Saint Médard, bishop of Noyon.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abbey-of-saint-victor-marseille"><span class="enc-name">Abbey of Saint-Victor, Marseille</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Abbey of Saint-Victor in Marseille, France, was founded around 415 by John Cassian near the tomb of the martyr Victor.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-al-bakri"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz al-Bakri</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz al-Bakri was the second and last ruler of the Taifa of Huelva and Saltés in south-western al-Andalus, a member of the Arab Bakri family.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-al-fishtali"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz al-Fishtali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz al-Fishtali was a Moroccan scholar, poet and court historian of the Saadi sultan Ahmad al-Mansur, in whose chancery he served as a leading secretary.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-abi-amir"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Amir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Amir, a grandson of the Córdoban regent al-Mansur, ruled the taifa of Valencia from 1021 to 1061.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-musa"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz ibn Musa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz ibn Musa was the son of Musa ibn Nusayr and the first Umayyad governor of al-Andalus, from 714 to 716.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-mutib-al-rashid"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz ibn Mutib Al Rashid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz ibn Mutib Al Rashid was emir of Jabal Shammar, ruled from Ha'il, from 1897 to 1906.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-aziz-ibn-shuayb"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Aziz ibn Shu'ayb</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Aziz ibn Shu'ayb was the last emir of the Emirate of Crete, ruling from about 949.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-hafid-of-morocco"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-hafid-of-morocco.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Hafid of Morocco</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Hafid was sultan of Morocco of the Alaouite dynasty from 1908 to 1912.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-haqq-ii"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Haqq II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Haqq II was the last Marinid sultan of Morocco, proclaimed as a baby in 1420 and kept under the control of Wattasid regents for most of his reign.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-ilah"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-ilah.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Ilah</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Ilah was regent of Iraq from 1939 to 1953 for his young nephew King Faisal II, and remained crown prince and a dominant influence afterwards.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-karim-ibn-jama"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Karim ibn Jama</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Karim ibn Jama was the founder of the Wadai Empire, traditionally dated to around 1611, when he led a successful revolt against the ruling Tunjur dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-karim-qasim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-karim-qasim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Karim Qasim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Karim Qasim was an Iraqi army officer who led the revolution of 14 July 1958 that overthrew the Hashemite monarchy.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-latif-mirza"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Latif Mirza</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Latif Mirza was a Timurid prince, the son of Ulugh Beg.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-i-saadi-sultan"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Malik I (Saadi sultan)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Malik I was a sultan of the Saadi dynasty of Morocco in the 1570s.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-atiyya"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Malik ibn Atiyya</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Malik ibn Atiyya was an Umayyad general sent by the caliph Marwan II with Syrian troops against the Ibadi rebels who had seized Mecca and Medina in 747.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-hudhayl"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Malik ibn Hudhayl</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Malik ibn Hudhayl was ruler of the Taifa of Albarracín, a mountain principality of eastern al-Andalus, from about 1045 to 1103, a member of the Berber Banu Razin…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-malik-ibn-marwan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan was an Umayyad caliph who reigned from 685 to 705.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-malik-imad-al-dawla"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Malik Imad al-Dawla was the last Hudid ruler of the Taifa of Zaragoza.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-mumin"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-mumin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Mu'min</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Mu'min was the first caliph of the Almohad Caliphate, proclaimed caliph c. 1133 as successor to the movement's founder Ibn Tumart.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-nabi-ibn-ali-ibn-mahdi"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Nabi ibn Ali ibn Mahdi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Nabi ibn Ali ibn Mahdi was the second ruler of the Mahdid dynasty of Yemen, succeeding his father, who had taken Zabid from the Najahids in 1159.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-rahman-al-ghafiqi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi was the Umayyad governor of al-Andalus in 721 and again from 730.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-rahman-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman I was an Umayyad prince who escaped the massacre of his family during the Abbasid Revolution and fled to al-Andalus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ibn-rustam"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman ibn Rustam</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman ibn Rustam was an Ibadi leader of Persian descent who governed Kairouan for the Ibadi Imamate of Tripolitania from 758.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-rahman-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman II was Umayyad emir of Córdoba from 822 to 852.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-iii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-rahman-iii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman III was the Umayyad emir of Córdoba from 912 who proclaimed himself caliph in 929, founding the Caliphate of Córdoba.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-sanchuelo"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rahman Sanchuelo was a son of Almanzor by a daughter of King Sancho II of Pamplona, hence his nickname 'Little Sancho'.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rashid-khan"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Rashid Khan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Rashid Khan (1508–1560) was the second khan of the Yarkent Khanate, succeeding his father Sultan Said Khan in 1533.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-wahid-ben-messaoud"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abd-al-wahid-ben-messaoud.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd al-Wahid ben Messaoud was a Moroccan envoy of the Saadi sultan Ahmad al-Mansur who led an embassy to the court of Elizabeth I of England in 1600.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-bayyasi"><span class="enc-name">Abd Allah al-Bayyasi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd Allah al-Bayyasi was an Almohad prince and governor in al-Andalus who proclaimed himself caliph at Baeza in 1224, during the succession crisis after the death of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-murtada"><span class="enc-name">Abd Allah al-Murtada</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd Allah al-Murtada was the governor of the Balearic Islands who declared himself independent at Madina Mayurqa, the later Palma, in 1076, when the Taifa of Dénia…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-al-uyuni"><span class="enc-name">Abd Allah al-Uyuni</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd Allah al-Uyuni was the founder of the Uyunid Emirate in eastern Arabia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/abd-allah-beg"><span class="enc-name">Abd Allah Beg</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Abd Allah Beg was the leader of the Kumul (Hami) oasis in eastern Xin